  "explanation": "NIST's OSCAL documentation draws a two-layer distinction between these terms.\n\n'Well-formed' refers to the syntactic correctness of the underlying data format (JSON, XML, or YAML) — that is, whether the file can be successfully parsed by a language runtime and its standard libraries. As the documentation states, [Q1] 'NIST's OSCAL tools do not explicitly check that JSON, XML, or YAML is well-formed. If you are a tool developer, we expect you to use your language runtime and libraries to parse JSON, XML, and YAML and check for the well-formed data. This must occur before validating an OSCAL document.' Well-formedness is therefore a precondition for validation, not part of it.\n\n'Valid' refers to the structural and semantic conformance of a parsed document against the OSCAL schemas (e.g., JSON Schema or XML Schema). Evidence 3 points to schema-based validators as the tools that perform this check [Q2].\n\nBoth properties together define whether software can treat a file as OSCAL at all. As NIST puts it, [Q3] 'Generally, both \"well-formedness\" and \"validity\" of OSCAL instance data determine if a conformant OSCAL application can process such data. In this way, \"well-formedness\" and \"validity\" define the boundary between what can be considered OSCAL data and what cannot.' NIST further emphasises why both matter: [Q4] 'Being able to validate documents against externally-specified schemas (mutually and generally understood) is a foundation of robust, secure data interchange and interoperability.'\n\nIn short: well-formed means the file parses without error in its native format; valid means the parsed content satisfies the OSCAL schema constraints. One check is done by the developer's parser; the other is done by a schema validator. The evidence does not supply the full verbatim definitions from the 'What is well-formed data?' and 'What is valid data?' sub-sections themselves, only the surrounding context quoted above.",
  "quotes": [
    {
      "id": "Q1",
      "source": "validation",
      "text": "NIST's OSCAL tools do not explicitly check that JSON, XML, or YAML is well-formed. If you are a tool developer, we expect you to use your language runtime and libraries to parse JSON, XML, and YAML and check for the well-formed data. This must occur before validating an OSCAL document."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q2",
      "source": "validation",
      "text": "There are many tools to validate documents using schema formats."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q3",
      "source": "validation",
      "text": "Generally, both \"well-formedness\" and \"validity\" of OSCAL instance data determine if a conformant OSCAL application can process such data. In this way, \"well-formedness\" and \"validity\" define the boundary between what can be considered OSCAL data and what cannot. The degree to which an application can support well-formed, valid OSCAL data defines how conformant the application is."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q4",
      "source": "validation",
      "text": "Being able to validate documents against externally-specified schemas (mutually and generally understood) is a foundation of robust, secure data interchange and interoperability."
    }
  ],
  "next_step": "If you want to check well-formedness, use your language's standard JSON/XML/YAML parser before invoking oscal-validate. For structural validity, oscal-validate applies the OSCAL schemas and constraint rules; review any findings it reported for specifics on what failed."
