  "explanation": "NIST requires last-modified in every OSCAL document's metadata for two related reasons: it is a mandatory structural field, and it serves a specific operational purpose for tooling.\n\nOn the structural side, NIST states that every document must carry it: [Q1] confirms that the metadata section \"must include a title, last-modified timestamp, and OSCAL syntax version,\" and [Q2] defines the field as \"The date and time that the document was last modified.\"\n\nOn the operational side, NIST explains that last-modified is one of two fast-change-detection signals for tools. [Q3] states: \"The last-modified field in metadata must be assigned with the date and time at the moment the file is saved with the modified content. These are two mechanisms by which tools can quickly 'know' if a file has changed since it was last encountered.\" The companion mechanism is the root-element UUID, which must also be regenerated on every content change. Together, these two fields let a tool detect document changes without parsing or comparing the full document body.\n\nNIST also notes the cross-format implication: [Q3] adds, \"When converting between formats, such as XML to JSON, these values should remain the same. This enables tools to know the content within the two formats is equivalent.\" This means last-modified is not just a version-control convenience; it is a cross-format equivalence signal as well.\n\nThis same requirement and rationale appear verbatim across multiple model-specific pages (assessment plan, assessment results, catalog), indicating it is a universal constraint applied to all OSCAL models, not a model-specific rule.",
