  "explanation": "An allowed-values constraint defines an enumerated set of permitted values for a field or flag. The @allow-other attribute controls whether that set is 'open' or 'closed'. [Q1] When @allow-other is set to 'yes', it marks the expected value set as open, meaning the enumerated values are treated as suggestions rather than strict requirements. When @allow-other is 'no' (which is the default when the attribute is omitted), the expected value set is closed. [Q2] The practical difference in processing is: for a closed set, 'the actual value MUST match one of the enumerated values declared on any of the <allowed-values> constraints in the targeting set. An error MUST be produced to indicate that the value doesn't match one of the enumerated values.' [Q3] For an open set, 'Any type-appropriate actual value MUST be allowed. A warning MAY be produced to indicate that the value doesn't match one of the enumerated values.' [Q4] Importantly, if multiple allowed-values constraints apply to the same target node (the 'applicable set'), even a single constraint in that set with @allow-other='no' overrides any others that have 'yes', making the whole set closed. [Q5] The example in the specification shows @allow-other='yes' on a flag constraint that lists 'laptop' and 'desktop' as enumerated values, meaning other form-factor values would also be structurally permitted. [Q6]",
